<h2>Why Temple Roofs Fail Where They Do</h2>
<p>Central Texas weather is hard on the weakest parts of a roof. Long stretches of summer heat in the upper 90s and 100s bake rubber seals and asphalt sealant strips until they crack or lose their grip. Spring storms then push wind-driven rain sideways under those weakened edges. That combination is why so many Temple leaks trace back to pipe boots, flashing and ridge areas rather than the open field of the roof.</p>
<p>Older homes near downtown Temple often have several generations of patches layered around chimneys and additions, and sorting those out takes patience. Newer subdivisions in west and south Temple tend to have fewer of those issues but can show problems at vents and valleys within the first decade, especially if attic ventilation is poor. Expansive clay soils can also shift a house enough over time to open gaps where walls meet the roofline. Heavy downpours that overwhelm clogged gutters can back water up under the first course of shingles, which is why we also check <a href="/services/gutters-roof-drainage-temple-tx/">gutters and roof drainage</a> during a repair visit.</p>

<h2>When to Schedule a Repair Inspection</h2>
<p>Call for an inspection as soon as you notice a ceiling stain, find shingle pieces in the yard, or see flashing that has pulled away. Small leaks soak insulation and decking long before they show indoors, and the repair is almost always simpler when caught early. It's also smart to have the roof checked after any severe storm that brought hail or high winds to your neighborhood; our <a href="/weather/">local weather page</a> can help you see what passed through. For older roofs, a check every year or two lets you plan repairs on your schedule. Our guide on <a href="/blog/common-causes-of-roof-leaks-central-texas/">common causes of roof leaks in Central Texas</a> covers the patterns we see most often.</p>
<p>Repairs are quoted individually because no two roofs are the same. The inspection is free, the estimate is in writing, and you decide what happens next.</p>
